# Project Guidelines and Rules

This document contains all project guidelines and rules for AI agents working on this codebase.

## Core Principles

### Do What's Asked
- Do what has been asked; nothing more, nothing less
- NEVER create files unless absolutely necessary for achieving your goal
- ALWAYS prefer editing an existing file to creating a new one
- NEVER proactively create documentation files (*.md) or README files unless explicitly requested

### Single Feature Focus
- Only work on one feature at a time
- Always commit changes whenever you update code
- Write well-documented code (self-documenting or commented)
- Explain decisions thoroughly to the user

## Task List Management

### Single Source of Truth
- Always check `TASK_LIST.md` before starting work and update it with any new tasks
- `TASK_LIST.md` is the single source of truth for all work items
- Never invent hidden TODOs or work on unlisted items

### Task Structure
- Each task should have a checked or unchecked box
- Tasks can have subtasks and notes for both finished and unfinished tasks
- For long explanations, create a separate `.md` file and reference it in the task
- You may only remove/delete unfinished tasks if they are no longer applicable

### Task Ordering
- Keep all completed items at the top of the list
- Append new completed tasks to the bottom of the completed section (chronological order: oldest → newest)
- Maintain three-section structure: "In Progress" → "Future" → "Completed"
- Never reorder completed tasks unless correcting an error

### Task Updates
- Move item to "In Progress" before starting work
- Update `TASK_LIST.md` in the same commit as the completed work
- Include detailed descriptions of what was accomplished
- Reference specific files, features, or technical details

## Feature Regression Prevention

### No Feature Regression Rule
Never delete, rename, or materially alter existing classes, routes, tests, or UI elements unless:
- a) The change is explicitly listed in `TASK_LIST.md` under "In Progress", AND
- b) A replacement of equal or greater capability ships in the same commit

### Change Impact Assessment
If carrying out a change would alter the structure or function of existing functionality:
- Verify before making the change
- Explain what and how the change would impact existing functionality
- Provide solutions to ensure modifications don't break working functionality

### Escalation Process
If unsure whether a change is a regression or impacts existing functionality:
- Create a `### Cursor-Next-Help-Wanted` entry
- Wait for feedback from another AI agent

### Protected Files
These files must never be deleted and modifications require explicit user approval:
- `TASK_LIST.md`
- `PROJECT_OVERVIEW.md`
- `IMPLEMENTATION_DETAILS.md`
- `CHANGELOG.md`
- All `.cursor/rules/*` files

### Project Context Preservation
- Never modify or remove project-specific language, privacy controls, or safety features without explicit user consent
- Preserve the core aesthetic and nature of the UI
- Never modify security rules, authentication flows, or user data schemas without explicit approval

## Git Practices

### Branching Strategy
Work on feature or fix branches named `<type>/<descriptive-slug>` (e.g., `feat/conversation-ui`, `fix/auth-cors`)

### Commit Message Format
```
<type>: <subject line>

Body (wrap at 72 chars) explaining WHY. WHAT is visible in the diff.

Updates TASK_LIST.md: [task description if applicable]
Updates CHANGELOG.md: [changelog entry description if applicable]
```

Valid types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `chore`, `test`, `style`

### Atomic Commits
- Each commit must compile, pass basic functionality tests, and keep the app runnable
- Never break existing features in a commit
- Always update `TASK_LIST.md` in the same commit when completing work

### Project Safety Commits
Any commit affecting user privacy, authentication, or core content must include:
- Clear explanation of privacy implications
- Confirmation that project safety is maintained
- Reference to relevant security considerations

## Testing Guidelines

### Manual Testing Requirements
For every new route, UI component, or authentication flow:
- Test manually in the appropriate environment
- Test API endpoints using relevant tools
- Verify authentication flows work end-to-end

### Pre-commit Verification
Before every commit, verify:
- The application starts without errors
- Main entry points open without console errors
- Authentication flow works (login/signup/logout)
- No broken links or missing resources

### Project Safety Testing
For changes affecting user data, privacy, or core content:
- Verify user data remains private and secure
- Test that core language remains appropriate
- Confirm no user data leaks in logs or error messages

### Future Test Structure
When formal tests are added, create:
- `tests/unit/` for individual component tests
- `tests/integration/` for API endpoint tests
- `tests/e2e/` for full user journey tests
- `tests/security/` for privacy and authentication tests

## Documentation Standards

### Update Documentation on Changes
If code, API routes, or UI changes, update related documentation in the same commit:
- `PROJECT_OVERVIEW.md` for high-level feature changes
- `IMPLEMENTATION_DETAILS.md` for technical architecture updates
- Setup files for deployment or configuration changes
- API route docstrings for endpoint modifications
- `CHANGELOG.md` for notable changes

### Documentation Requirements
- Use clear, descriptive docstrings for all API endpoints with privacy implications
- Keep configuration examples updated with all required environment variables
- Include clear descriptions and security notes for sensitive configurations
- Update architecture documentation when adding new components or modifying schemas

### Project Documentation Standards
All user-facing text, error messages, and core content must:
- Use appropriate, non-judgmental language
- Maintain the established tone
- Include privacy and safety considerations
- Reference best practices where applicable

## Changelog Management

### Structure
- Maintain `[Unreleased]` section for ongoing changes
- Use versioned sections like `[Sprint X] - YYYY-MM-DD` or `[vX.Y.Z] - YYYY-MM-DD`
- Include subsections: `### Added`, `### Changed`, `### Deprecated`, `### Removed`, `### Fixed`, `### Security`

### When to Update
- After completing a task: Append entry under `[Unreleased]` with task ID reference
- At sprint/milestone end: Organize `[Unreleased]` entries into new versioned section
- In the same commit as `TASK_LIST.md` updates for completed work

### Best Practices
- Link to external resources like Keep a Changelog in file header
- Use markdown for readability
- Include dates in ISO format (YYYY-MM-DD)
- Mark completions with ✅ where appropriate
- Never remove historical entries

## Module Size Guidelines

### Target Size
- Aim for modules (files) to be 500-1000 lines of code
- Consider breaking down modules exceeding 1000 lines

### When to Split
Look for natural boundaries such as:
- Separate concerns or functionalities
- Different components
- Each new module should have a clear, well-defined purpose

### Signs a Module is Too Large
- Understanding the module's purpose is difficult at a glance
- Frequent scrolling or jumping between parts is required
- High mental effort needed to remember context between different parts

### Refactoring Process
- Identify cohesive groups of functions, classes, or components
- Create folders and regroup specialized features into individual modules
- Maintain clear responsibilities and interfaces
- Update documentation to reflect module structure changes

## Handshake Protocol

When needing input from another AI agent, structure the message exactly as:

```markdown
### Cursor-Summary
<concise, 3–10 sentence summary of current situation>

### Cursor-Diff-Stat
<`git diff --stat` output OR "n/a">

### Cursor-Next-Help-Wanted
<clear questions or "none">
```

Requirements:
- Headings must not be changed
- Omit `Cursor-Diff-Stat` only if no files were touched
- Always include context about which part of the project architecture is being discussed
- Reference relevant `TASK_LIST.md` items and their current status 